Uganda's leading independent media group said it was under "military siege" on Sunday after the army chief ordered the shutdown of its newspapers, TV station and radio outlets. This comes amid a widespread crackdown on free speech, initiated by President Yoweri Museveni's son.   
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/198e8cec-6987-11f1-ba6f-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-B2Q638G.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/198e8cec-6987-11f1-ba6f-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-B2Q638G.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d8e138a2-72d2-11f1-b9bb-31dfa77f3cba</guid><pubDate>Sun, 28 Jun 2026 09:34:59 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/198e8cec-6987-11f1-ba6f-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-B2Q638G.jpg">AFP - BADRU KATUMBA</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>France</category><title>Remains of people exhibited in ‘human zoos’ to be returned to French Guiana</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/france/20260628-remains-of-people-exhibited-in-human-zoos-to-be-returned-to-french-guiana</link><description>
In 1892, around 30 men, women and children from the Kali’na and Arawak peoples of French Guiana were brought to Paris to be put on display at "human zoos". Six of them did not survive the winter. A bill allowing the repatriation of their remains to their homeland has now received final approval – the culmination of a long struggle waged by their descendants.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/064212e0-6828-11f1-99d8-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/Chamane.jpeg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/064212e0-6828-11f1-99d8-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/Chamane.jpeg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">bb6bf5d8-6e35-11f1-8f95-7ba779e4a441</guid><pubDate>Sun, 28 Jun 2026 12:24:14 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/064212e0-6828-11f1-99d8-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/Chamane.jpeg">© Arame Mbengue / RFI</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>France</category><title>New Caledonia election tests territory's future after unrest and deadlock</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/france/20260628-new-caledonia-election-tests-territory-s-future-after-unrest-and-deadlock</link><description>
Polling stations have just closed in New Caledonia following provincial elections in a vote that will test the French Pacific territory's future after years of political deadlock and unrest.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/17b9fc4e-72c4-11f1-ae9c-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/People-voting-in-New-Caledonia-28-June-2026-AFP-Delphine-Mayeur.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/17b9fc4e-72c4-11f1-ae9c-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/People-voting-in-New-Caledonia-28-June-2026-AFP-Delphine-Mayeur.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1c887e44-6fb3-11f1-bfde-895fd0b87d64</guid><pubDate>Sun, 28 Jun 2026 07:26:25 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/17b9fc4e-72c4-11f1-ae9c-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/People-voting-in-New-Caledonia-28-June-2026-AFP-Delphine-Mayeur.jpg">AFP - DELPHINE MAYEUR</source><dc:creator>Alison Hird</dc:creator></item><item><category>International</category><title>European heatwave continues to top records amid high alerts in the east</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/international/20260627-european-heatwave-continues-to-top-records-amid-high-alerts-in-the-east</link><description>
Tens of millions face a weekend of extreme temperatures in Europe as a deadly heatwave moves eastwards, with German forecasters warning that more records could be broken and eastern countries issuing a slew of red alerts for the coming days.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/90fa4050-713e-11f1-9bde-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-25T151834Z-985103065-RC231MAW2D7O-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-FRANCE-HEAT.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/90fa4050-713e-11f1-9bde-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-25T151834Z-985103065-RC231MAW2D7O-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-FRANCE-HEAT.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">beea3ec0-7242-11f1-9935-6f8bbe7fb1bc</guid><pubDate>Sat, 27 Jun 2026 16:10:41 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/90fa4050-713e-11f1-9bde-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-25T151834Z-985103065-RC231MAW2D7O-RTRMADP-3-EUROPE-WEATHER-FRANCE-HEAT.jpg">REUTERS - Gonzalo Fuentes</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Environment</category><title>Marine biobanks: the science of 'stopping time' for an ecosystem under pressure</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/environment/20260627-marine-biobanks-the-science-of-stopping-time-for-an-ocean-under-pressure</link><description>
In labs around the world, the ocean is being kept in jars. Some of it is alive, bubbling in flasks, while some lies frozen in liquid nitrogen. These marine biobanks are collections of coral cells, algae, bacteria and other living materials that scientists are working to preserve before warming seas and disappearing habitats consume them.
